<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José de Iturrigaray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1742">1742</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José de Iturrigaray was a Spanish army officer and Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> from 1803 to 1808. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> forced the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> to abdicate in 1808, he appeared to favour criollo proposals for a provisional assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and peninsular merchants led by Gabriel de Yermo overthrew him in September 1808. The coup deepened the split between Spaniards and criollos that preceded the war of independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-de-iturrigaray.jpg" alt="José de Iturrigaray" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viceroy_Jos%C3%A9_de_Iturrigaray.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Joaquín Vicente de Iturrigaray y Aróstegui was born in Cádiz on 27 June 1742 into a family of wealthy Basque merchants; both his parents came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. Under Charles III he took part in the Spanish invasion of Portugal in 1762 and in operations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, and under Charles IV he won a name for courage in the War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> against Republican France in 1793. He was governor of Cádiz from 1793 to 1798, and in 1801 he commanded the army of Andalucía in the War of the Oranges with Portugal under Manuel de Godoy, who then named him viceroy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Iturrigaray reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> in 1803 with his wife, María Inés de Jáuregui, a daughter of a viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and brought in a large shipment of goods free of duty by declaring it his personal baggage. His friendly, jovial manner made him popular after his austere predecessor, Félix Berenguer de Marquina. On 9 December 1803 he inaugurated Manuel Tolsá's bronze equestrian statue of Charles IV in the Zócalo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, and he aided the research of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a>, who spent a year in the colony.</p><p>When war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was renewed in 1805, he was ordered to put the colony in a state of defence and to send more money to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He applied the Cédula de la Caja de Consolidación, which transferred income from church estates and foundations to the government and drew protests from, among others, Bishop Manuel Abad y Queipo. Higher taxes spread popular discontent, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsulares">Peninsulares</a> resented the favour he showed the Criollos, on whom he came to rely.</p><p>The conspirators who seized him in the early hours of 15 September 1808 numbered five hundred, and one soldier was killed. He was taken to the Inquisition, so that the public would believe he had been removed for heresy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> deposed him. An inventory of his valuables came to more than one million pesos, which was taken as evidence that he had enriched himself. Marshal Pedro de Garibay, an octogenarian controlled by the Audiencia, replaced him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as a prisoner on 21 September 1808, he was tried in Cádiz for disloyalty. The charges were not proven, and he was freed under the amnesty granted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> in 1810. A juicio de residencia went on until his death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 22 August 1815, and he was fined 435,000 pesetas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José de Iturrigaray” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Napoleon forced the Spanish Bourbons to abdicate in 1808, Spanish merchants in Mexico City overthrew Viceroy <b>José de Iturrigaray</b> to prevent criollo moves towards self-government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cádiz. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1789" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of José de Iturrigaray"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4759 4110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4759 4110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1789">What was here in 1789? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
